--TEST--
`Core\Config::get` with a secret directive written as its name returns a `?secret string`, so `echo`, a `??` over it and a plain `string` variable refuse it, while any other setting stays a `?string` — `rule:config/a-secret-is-a-file-whose-content-is-the-value`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `db.main.password` and `http.csrf_key` are secret settings. The result of
// `get` for them is a `?secret string`, which no output can print.
echo Core\Config::get('db.main.password');
echo Core\Config::get('http.csrf_key') ?? 'none';
string $password = Core\Config::get(name: 'mail.relay.password') ?? '';

// `db.main.host` is not secret, so its result is a plain `?string`.
echo Core\Config::get('db.main.host') ?? 'none';
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0790]: `echo` cannot print a `secret` value
  --> case.nvs:4:6
%A
error[E0790]: `echo` cannot print a `secret` value
  --> case.nvs:5:6
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `string|secret string`
  --> case.nvs:6:20
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
%A
